      <div class="key-points">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Baijiu (白酒, "white liquor") is a Chinese grain spirit, typically 38–53% ABV, distilled from sorghum, wheat, rice, corn, or barley.</li>
          <li>It is fermented <strong>solid</strong>, not in liquid. Grain stays in a damp heap with a microbial starter called <em>qu</em>, which is why the aroma is so much denser than vodka's.</li>
          <li>Aroma type — sauce, strong, light, rice, and several smaller categories — predicts flavour far better than price or packaging.</li>
          <li>Most baijiu is drunk at room temperature, in small cups, with food. It is a table spirit, not a shot spirit.</li>
          <li>Outside China, expect to pay $15–60 for a solid entry bottle, $60–150 for a recognised premium name, and $300+ for Moutai-tier prestige.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

