      <h2 id="what">What baijiu actually is</h2>
      <p>Baijiu is a distilled spirit made from grain, fermented with a mixed microbial starter, and produced almost entirely in China. The legal definition matters more than the marketing: under Chinese national standards, baijiu is a distilled liquor made from grains through fermentation, distillation, ageing, and blending. The grain bill, the starter, the fermentation vessel, and the ageing container are what separate one bottle from another.</p>
      <p>Three things make it structurally different from every other major spirit category:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It is fermented as a solid.</strong> Whisky, rum, and vodka ferment in liquid wash. Baijiu ferments grain in a damp, porous heap or pit, with 55–65% moisture. That environment supports moulds and bacteria that a liquid ferment cannot sustain.</li>
        <li><strong>The starter is a solid cake of mould-cultured grain.</strong> <em>Qu</em> (曲) is not a yeast packet. It is a brick of wheat, barley, or rice colonised by moulds, yeasts, and bacteria, and it determines the aroma family before fermentation even begins.</li>
        <li><strong>It is distilled in batches, then blended across years and vessels.</strong> A single premium bottle may combine base spirits from dozens of fermentation pits and several vintages. Blending is the master distiller's main craft.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That combination produces aroma compounds in concentrations that surprise drinkers coming from whisky or gin. A good strong-aroma bottle throws ripe pineapple and honey at you. A sauce-aroma bottle smells of fermented soybean, roasted grain, and dried mushrooms. Neither is an additive. Both come from microbes.</p>

