      <h2 id="qu">Qu: the one idea that explains the taste</h2>
      <p>If you remember one thing about baijiu, remember <em>qu</em>. Everything else — the aroma, the body, the price tiers, the regional styles — traces back to it.</p>
      <p>Qu is made by pressing a damp grain-and-water mixture into bricks, inoculating it with a culture, and letting it sit in a warm room until moulds colonise the whole brick. The temperature it reaches during that incubation decides what kind of qu it becomes, and that decides what kind of baijiu you get:</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Qu type</th><th>Peak temperature</th><th>Made from</th><th>Typical use</th><th>Aroma signature it creates</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Low-temperature daqu</td><td>40–50 °C</td><td>Barley, peas</td><td>Light aroma</td><td>Clean grain, pear, faint florals</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Medium-temperature daqu</td><td>50–60 °C</td><td>Wheat</td><td>Strong aroma</td><td>Ripe fruit, honey, cellar</td></tr>
            <tr><td>High-temperature daqu</td><td>60–65 °C</td><td>Wheat</td><td>Sauce aroma</td><td>Savory, roasted, soy, nuts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Xiaoqu (small qu)</td><td>25–35 °C</td><td>Rice bran, herbs</td><td>Rice aroma</td><td>Soft, floral, lightly sweet</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Fuqu (bran qu)</td><td>Controlled</td><td>Bran, cultured</td><td>Entry-level bottles</td><td>Lighter, simpler, faster</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The practical consequence: when a bottle says "sauce aroma," it is telling you the producer used high-temperature qu, fermented in stone-lined pits, and ran a year-long cycle. When it says "light aroma," it is telling you the opposite — low-temperature qu, earthenware jars, a fast double-fermentation. You can predict the glass from the label.</p>
      <p>This is also why <a href="/db/">the database</a> is organised by aroma type rather than by brand. A brand tells you who made it. The aroma type tells you what it will taste like.</p>

      <blockquote><p>Qu is a living ecosystem, not an ingredient. Two distilleries using the same grain and the same water will produce different baijiu if their qu differs — and their qu always differs.</p></blockquote>

      <h2 id="aroma">The six aroma types</h2>
      <p>Chinese standards recognise twelve aroma types. Six of them account for almost everything you will find on a shelf outside China, and they are the ones worth learning first.</p>

