      <h3>Which one should you start with?</h3>
      <p>For a first bottle, light aroma or an approachable strong aroma is usually the safer bet. Light aroma is the most legible style for a palate trained on whisky or gin — it is dry, grain-forward, and does not ask you to enjoy fermented soybean. Strong aroma is the crowd-pleaser of the category: fruit and honey make it immediately likeable, and it holds up to rich food.</p>
      <p>Sauce aroma is the style everyone has heard of, and the worst place to begin. Its savoury intensity is genuinely divisive. Drinkers who love peated Islay whisky, mezcal, or funky Jamaican rum tend to connect with it faster than drinkers who prefer clean spirits. Everyone else should arrive after five or ten tastings, not before. Our <a href="/best-baijiu-for-beginners/">beginner bottle guide</a> ranks specific options by aroma type and price.</p>

      <h2 id="made">How a bottle is made</h2>
      <p>Production differs by aroma type, but the general arc is the same: grain preparation, qu inoculation, solid-state fermentation, batch distillation, ageing, blending.</p>

      <h3>1. Grain and qu</h3>
      <p>Sorghum is the backbone of most baijiu. It is high in starch, tolerant of the heat and acidity of solid-state fermentation, and contributes a characteristic grain sweetness. Wheat, rice, glutinous rice, corn, barley, and peas appear in various combinations. The five-grain formula used by <a href="/db/variety/wuliangye/">Wuliangye</a> — sorghum, rice, glutinous rice, wheat, corn — is the most famous example of deliberate grain blending, with each grain contributing a different compound: sorghum for structure, rice for cleanliness, glutinous rice for body, wheat for aroma, corn for fruity esters.</p>

      <h3>2. Fermentation</h3>
      <p>The vessel defines the style. Strong aroma ferments in mud pits, where the pit wall's microbial community — built up over decades — contributes the cellar note. Sauce aroma ferments in stone-lined pits in a year-long cycle of repeated steaming and fermentation. Light aroma ferments in earthenware jars set into the ground, kept separate from soil contact, which is why it stays clean.</p>
      <p>Fermentation length varies enormously. Strong aroma runs 60–90 days per batch. Sauce aroma runs a full year, with eight fermentation rounds and seven separate distillate collections.</p>

      <h3>3. Distillation</h3>
      <p>Traditional pot stills, run in batches. The distiller separates the heads, heart, and tails by strength and taste, keeping the heart. This is closer to Cognac production than to column-still vodka.</p>

      <h3>4. Ageing</h3>
      <p>Ceramic jars, usually unglazed earthenware, in a cool cellar. The jar is slightly porous, so oxygen slowly crosses the wall and the spirit breathes. Three years is a common minimum for premium lines; sauce aroma typically ages base spirit for at least three years before blending, then rests the blend further. Ageing softens the raw edge and builds the roundness that separates a good bottle from a harsh one.</p>

      <h3>5. Blending</h3>
      <p>Almost no premium baijiu is a single-vessel product. The blender combines base spirits from different pits, different fermentation rounds, and different years to hit a consistent house profile. This is why "vintage" claims on baijiu labels mean something different from whisky: the number usually refers to the age of a component or the year of a commemorative release, not to a single-barrel bottling.</p>

      <h2 id="abv">Why 52% and 53% keep appearing</h2>
      <p>Two numbers dominate Chinese shelves: 52% for strong aroma and 53% for sauce aroma. These are conventions, not laws, and the reasons are part tradition and part chemistry.</p>
      <p>At around 52–54% ABV, ethanol and water molecules associate most tightly in solution, which tasters describe as a rounder, less prickly mouthfeel. Whether the effect is as pronounced as the industry claims is debatable, but the practical outcome is real: a well-made 52% strong aroma often feels softer than a 40% spirit of lesser quality, because the aroma compounds are carried differently and the body is fuller.</p>
      <p>Lower-strength versions exist and are popular. Yanghe's flagship sits at 40.8%, Luzhou Laojiao sells a 38% expression, and Fenjiu offers 42% and 48% alongside its 53%. Lower ABV is easier to drink neat and better for long meals, at the cost of some aromatic intensity. Neither is superior — they are different products for different tables.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Reading the label:</strong> ABV is printed as <em>vol</em> (酒精度). A bottle marked 52% vol contains 52 ml of ethanol per 100 ml. Chinese menus and gift boxes often quote the number without the % sign, so "52度" means 52% ABV.</div>

      <h2 id="drink">How to drink it without wasting it</h2>
      <p>Baijiu is served at room temperature in small cups — typically 15–25 ml, sometimes smaller. The cup size is not a challenge. It exists because the aroma is intense and because the spirit is meant to be sipped alongside food over a long meal.</p>
      <p>The sequence that works:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pour small.</strong> 15 ml is enough to evaluate. You can always pour again.</li>
        <li><strong>Smell before you sip.</strong> Hold the cup still at chin height. Baijiu's volatile compounds are dense; aggressive swirling releases alcohol rather than aroma.</li>
        <li><strong>Sip, do not shoot.</strong> A small mouthful, held briefly. The first impression is often heat; the flavour arrives after.</li>
        <li><strong>Eat.</strong> This is the step most beginners skip. Baijiu is built for food — fat, salt, chilli, and fermented flavours all make the spirit read as intentional rather than harsh.</li>
        <li><strong>Notice the finish.</strong> Good baijiu lingers. A sauce-aroma bottle can hold for a minute or more, evolving from savoury to sweet.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Water is normal and expected. So is not finishing a glass. Chinese banquet culture has strong toasting conventions, but they are social rituals with room for pacing, and no host expects a guest to damage themselves. Our <a href="/how-to-drink-baijiu/">serving guide</a> covers glassware, temperature, food pairing by aroma type, and how to handle a toast-heavy dinner.</p>

