      <p>Two warnings. First, price and quality correlate weakly in baijiu, because a large part of the premium is gift status rather than liquid. A $40 bottle from a serious regional house frequently outperforms a $200 gift box at the table. Second, the most counterfeited bottles are the expensive ones, which means the high end carries real fraud risk. See <a href="/how-to-spot-fake-maotai/">how to spot fake Moutai</a> before spending above $150.</p>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Six mistakes beginners make</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Treating it as Chinese vodka.</strong> Vodka is engineered for neutrality. Baijiu is engineered for aroma. Expecting a clean, mixable spirit guarantees disappointment.</li>
        <li><strong>Starting with the most famous bottle.</strong> Moutai is a superb reference point and a poor first purchase. Its intensity and price both work against a beginner.</li>
        <li><strong>Drinking it cold or over ice.</strong> Chilling suppresses the aroma compounds that justify the price. Room temperature is the default for a reason.</li>
        <li><strong>Drinking it without food.</strong> Neat baijiu on an empty table is a hard introduction. The same bottle with roast meat or chilli oil is a different drink.</li>
        <li><strong>Buying by box weight.</strong> Heavy packaging signals gift positioning, not quality. Some of the best-value bottles come in plain boxes.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring the standard code.</strong> The GB/T number on the back label tells you the production method and aroma type. It is the single most reliable piece of information on the bottle.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout warning"><strong>On "pure grain" claims:</strong> Chinese labels distinguish 固态法 (solid-state, GB/T 10781 series and GB/T 26760), 固液法 (blended, GB/T 20822), and 液态法 (liquid-fermented, GB/T 20821). Only the first is what drinkers mean by traditional pure-grain baijiu. If the standard code starts with 20821 or 20822, the bottle is not solid-state regardless of what the front label implies.</div>

      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Is baijiu stronger than vodka?</h3>
        <p>Often, yes — 52–53% is common versus 40% for most vodka — but strength is not the main difference. Aroma intensity is. A 52% baijiu delivers far more aromatic impact per sip than a 40% vodka, which is why it reads as more powerful even when the numbers are close.</p>
        <h3>Does baijiu contain gluten?</h3>
        <p>Most baijiu is made from sorghum and wheat, both of which contain gluten proteins. Distillation removes proteins, so distilled spirits are generally considered gluten-free, but producers rarely certify this and cross-contamination in blending is possible. Anyone with coeliac disease should contact the producer directly rather than rely on general rules.</p>
        <h3>Why does baijiu smell so strong?</h3>
        <p>Solid-state fermentation with qu produces high concentrations of esters, acids, and sulphur compounds that liquid fermentation does not. Those compounds are the point. The same chemistry that makes a sauce-aroma bottle smell of fermented soybean makes a strong-aroma bottle smell of pineapple.</p>
        <h3>Can you mix baijiu into cocktails?</h3>
        <p>Yes, with care. Light and rice aroma work well as base spirits because they are cleaner. Strong aroma works in small proportions as a modifier. Sauce aroma overwhelms almost everything — treat it as a seasoning, not a base. See our <a href="/baijiu-vs-vodka/">baijiu vs vodka comparison</a> for the substitution logic.</p>
        <h3>How long does an open bottle keep?</h3>
        <p>Six to twelve months if tightly sealed and stored upright away from light and heat. Baijiu does not spoil, but it does lose aromatic intensity as oxygen works on the esters. Decanting into a smaller bottle reduces headspace and slows the decline.</p>
        <h3>Is older baijiu always better?</h3>
        <p>No. Ageing improves spirit that was well made to begin with. It cannot rescue a flawed base, and beyond a certain point — often 15–20 years in a standard bottle — further ageing adds little. Vintage claims are also frequently about the release year rather than the age of the liquid.</p>
